            <ArticleTitle>An unusual complication of tubal anastomosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>624-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report an unusual complication associated with the use of a long-term intrafallopian stent during microsurgical tubal anastomosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary academic center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 36-year-old woman in whom an intrafallopian stent used during a sterilization reversal procedure could not be transcervically retrieved in the office.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hysteroscopic evaluation for removal of intrafallopian stent, followed by operative laparoscopy for postoperative abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patient symptoms, potential for morbidity, and review of the literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Hysteroscopic view of the uterine cavity failed to identify the intrafallopian stent. Laporoscopic evaluation of postoperative abdominal pain revealed significant formation of pelvic and abdominal adhesions. The 2-0 nylon suture used as an intrafallopian stent was seen sitting freely on top of the liver serosa. Adhesiolysis and successful retrieval of the stent resolved the patient's symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To our knowledge, this is the first report describing complete dislodgment and cephalad migration of an intrafallopian stent. Patient morbidity and health care costs may increase when long-term stents are used for sterilization reversal.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
